<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Dillenburg</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Dillenburg is a town in central <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hesse">Hesse</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a>, on the Dill. Its castle, built in the 13th century, became the main residence of the Ottonian counts of Nassau, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/william-the-silent">William the Silent</a> was born there in 1533. The castle was destroyed during the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seven-years-war">Seven Years' War</a> in 1760, and only its casemates and the 19th-century William Tower remain.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/dillenburg.jpg" alt="Dillenburg" width="320" height="213" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Otto Domes · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Dillenburg_Schlo%C3%9Fberg_Gesamtanlage.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Dillenburg lies on the eastern edge of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/westerwald">Westerwald</a> range, in the narrow valley of the Dill, which flows from the border of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hesse">Hesse</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/westphalia">Westphalia</a> to Wetzlar and empties into the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lahn">Lahn</a>. The Dietzhölze flows into the Dill in the town. It was formerly the seat of the old Dillkreis district, now part of the Lahn-Dill-Kreis, and it lies on the Orange Route, a German-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a> holiday road that joins places associated with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/house-of-orange-nassau">House of Orange-Nassau</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Dillenburg is first mentioned in a document of 1254 and was the ancestral seat of the Orange branch of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/house-of-nassau">House of Nassau</a>. The first castle on the Schlossberg was of wood, so that no pictures of it exist, and it was destroyed in the Dernbacher Feud. William I of Orange-Nassau, who was born in Dillenburg, organised the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a> resistance against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a> from exile between 1567 and 1572. The land was administered by the presidents of the House of Nassau-Dillenburg. In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seven-years-war">Seven Years' War</a> the stately home was destroyed in 1760, and a street, Wilhelmstraße, was built out of its remains.</p><p>In 1797 one of the earliest schools of forestry in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a>, founded a decade earlier at Hungen by Georg Ludwig Hartig, was moved to Dillenburg. It continued there until 1805, when Hartig lost his position as Inspector of Forests for the Prince of Orange-Nassau after <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/napoleon">Napoleon</a> dissolved the principality.</p><p>In the 19th century the building of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/deutz">Deutz</a>–<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/giessen">Gießen</a> railway and the use of iron ore found on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lahn">Lahn</a>, Dill and Sieg brought the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/industrial-revolution">Industrial Revolution</a> to the region, with many mines, foundries and metalworking works. Branch lines were built from Dillenburg to places such as Gönnern and Ewersbach. In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-world-war">Second World War</a> the town was a target of Allied attacks because of its marshalling yard, and Niederscheld, now part of the town, was bombed because parts for the V-2 rocket were built at the Adolfshütte. Ore mining later became ever less profitable, and the last blast furnace, in Oberscheld, ceased operations.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>The Wilhelmsturm, built between 1872 and 1875 on the Schlossberg, is the landmark of the town and houses the Orange-Nassau Museum. The casemates beneath the former castle, from the 16th century, are among the biggest defensive works in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a>, and parts of them have been excavated and may be toured. Other buildings include the Evangelical Town Church of 1491, the Dillturm of 1597 and the Untertor, or Lower Gate, of 1344, with alterations in 1594 and 1737. The town is twinned with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/breda">Breda</a>, Diest, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hereford">Hereford</a> and Orange. In November 2017 its name was officially extended to Oranienstadt Dillenburg, to recall its connection with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/house-of-orange-nassau">House of Orange-Nassau</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Dillenburg” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Walram took the south, while Otto I received the northern lands with Siegen, <b>Dillenburg</b> and Herborn, founding the Ottonian line.</blockquote><a href="/state/county-of-nassau-dillenburg">County of Nassau-Dillenburg</a></li></ul></section>
